  "worked_solution": "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y-z}{3},\\,(y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,1\\le z\\le 3},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14119930/assets/3f06a9c686a935cb6c561affbcd224045618d9cacaf94eb7c9eab8a9ca8da44c.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y-z}{3})=\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{y-z}{3})=-\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{9}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
